<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden calf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sin of the calf, Egel hazahav</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The golden calf is a cult image that, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> made while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> was on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a>. The narrative says Aaron collected gold earrings and ornaments and fashioned the calf; Moses, on seeing it, broke the tablets, burned the calf, and the Levites killed some three thousand people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> also tells of a calf made in his absence. Scholars note that bull imagery was widespread in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a> and compare Exodus 32 with the golden calves of 1 Kings 12. The apostle Paul cites the episode in 1 Corinthians as a warning against idolatry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-calf.jpg" alt="Golden calf" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_poussin,_adorazione_del_vitello_d'oro,_1633-34FXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jeroboam ruled first from Shechem and later from Tirzah, and he set up royal sanctuaries at Bethel and Dan, each with a <b>golden calf</b>, so that his subjects need not go on pilgrimage to the Temple in Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
